The hyperfine splitting is given as a frequency difference Δν = 15.5 MHz.  
For an electron spin the resonance condition is ν = (g μB/h) B, so the field splitting is  

\[
\Delta B = \frac{\Delta\nu\,h}{g\mu_B}
        = \frac{15.5\times10^{6}\,\text{s}^{-1}\;(6.626\times10^{-34}\,\text{J·s})}
               {2.0033\;(9.274\times10^{-24}\,\text{J T}^{-1})}
        = 5.53\times10^{-4}\,\text{T}=0.553\;\text{mT}.
\]

Since 1 T = 10 000 G, this equals 5.53 G.  

To express the splitting in wavenumbers, use ΔE = hΔν and ΔE = hc · (cm⁻¹):

\[
\tilde{\nu}= \frac{\Delta\nu}{c}
            = \frac{15.5\times10^{6}\,\text{s}^{-1}}
                   {2.998\times10^{10}\,\text{cm s}^{-1}}
            = 5.17\times10^{-4}\,\text{cm}^{-1}.
\]

Thus the correct set of conversions is:

**0.553 mT = 5.530 G = 5.17 × 10⁻⁴ cm⁻¹**.

ANSWER: A

