The electron starts from rest, moves \(d=10\;\text{cm}=0.10\;{\rm m}\) in \(t=10^{-7}\;{\rm s}\).

\[
a=\frac{2d}{t^{2}}=\frac{2(0.10)}{(10^{-7})^{2}}=2.0\times10^{13}\;{\rm m\,s^{-2}}
\]

Force on the electron  

\[
F=m_{e}a=(9.11\times10^{-31}\;{\rm kg})(2.0\times10^{13})=1.82\times10^{-17}\;{\rm N}
\]

Electric field (SI)

\[
E_{\rm SI}= \frac{F}{e}= \frac{1.82\times10^{-17}}{1.60\times10^{-19}}
          \approx 1.14\times10^{2}\;{\rm V\,m^{-1}}
\]

Voltage between the points  

\[
V = E_{\rm SI}\,d = (1.14\times10^{2})(0.10) \approx 1.14\times10^{1}\;{\rm V}
     \approx 11.4\;{\rm V}
\]

To express the field in Gaussian units, use \(1\;{\rm statV\,cm^{-1}} = 3.0\times10^{4}\;{\rm V\,m^{-1}}\):

\[
E_{\rm cgs}= \frac{E_{\rm SI}}{3.0\times10^{4}}
            \approx 3.8\times10^{-3}\;{\rm statV\,cm^{-1}}
\]

Thus the field is \(3.8\times10^{-3}\;{\rm statV/cm}\) and the potential difference is \(11.4\;{\rm V}\).

**ANSWER: B**

