The threshold wavelength is  

\[
\lambda_{\text{th}}=\frac{hc}{\phi},\qquad hc\approx1.99\times10^{-25}\ \text{J·m}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Cs: } &\lambda_{\text{th}}=\frac{1.99\times10^{-25}}{3.2\times10^{-19}}=6.2\times10^{-7}\text{ m}=622\text{ nm}\\
\text{K: }  &\lambda_{\text{th}}=\frac{1.99\times10^{-25}}{3.6\times10^{-19}}=5.5\times10^{-7}\text{ m}=553\text{ nm}\\
\text{Cu: } &\lambda_{\text{th}}=\frac{1.99\times10^{-25}}{6.4\times10^{-19}}=3.1\times10^{-7}\text{ m}=311\text{ nm}\\
\text{Zn: } &\lambda_{\text{th}}=\frac{1.99\times10^{-25}}{5.8\times10^{-19}}=3.4\times10^{-7}\text{ m}=343\text{ nm}
\end{aligned}
\]

Visible light spans 380–750 nm. A metal will emit only if the incident photon wavelength is **shorter** than its \(\lambda_{\text{th}}\). Cs (622 nm) and K (553 nm) satisfy this for part of the visible spectrum, whereas Cu (311 nm) and Zn (343 nm) require ultraviolet photons. Thus Cu and Zn will not emit photoelectrons under visible illumination.

