The pressure jump across a curved gas–liquid interface follows Laplace’s law  

\[
\Delta p = \frac{2\sigma}{r}
\]

where \(\sigma\) is the surface‑tension coefficient and \(r\) the radius of curvature of the bubble.  

Given  

\[
\sigma = 72.7\times10^{-3}\ \text{N m}^{-1},\qquad 
d = 2\ \text{mm}\;\Rightarrow\; r = \frac{d}{2}=1.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{m}
\]

\[
\Delta p = \frac{2(72.7\times10^{-3})}{1.0\times10^{-3}}
        = \frac{0.1454}{1.0\times10^{-3}}
        \approx 1.45\times10^{2}\ \text{Pa}
\]

i.e. about \(1.44\times10^{2}\ \text{N m}^{-2}\). The closest value among the options is 143.4.

The gas in a bubble must be at a higher pressure than the surrounding liquid by the Laplace pressure for a spherical interface:

\[
\Delta p = \frac{2\sigma}{r}= \frac{4\sigma}{d}
\]

Given the surface tension of water   
\(\sigma = 72.7\times10^{-3}\ \text{N·m}^{-1}=0.0727\ \text{N·m}^{-1}\)  

and the desired bubble diameter \(d = 2\ \text{mm}=2\times10^{-3}\ \text{m}\),

\[
\Delta p = \frac{4(0.0727)}{2\times10^{-3}}
          = \frac{0.2908}{0.002}
          \approx 1.45\times10^{2}\ \text{Pa}=145\ \text{N·m}^{-2}
\]

Rounded to the values given, this corresponds to about **\(1.4\times10^{2}\ \text{Pa}\)**, which matches option B (143.4 N·m⁻¹, the units are a typographical error; they represent Pa).

