The intensity (average power per unit area) of a plane electromagnetic wave is  

\[
I=\frac{1}{2}\,c\varepsilon_{0}E_{0}^{2}
\]

where \(E_{0}\) is the electric‑field amplitude, \(c=3.0\times10^{8}\,\text{m s}^{-1}\) and  
\(\varepsilon_{0}=8.85\times10^{-12}\,\text{F m}^{-1}\).

\[
E_{0}= \sqrt{\frac{2I}{c\varepsilon_{0}}}
     = \sqrt{\frac{2(1.35\times10^{3}\,\text{W m}^{-2})}
                 {(3.0\times10^{8})(8.85\times10^{-12})}}
     \approx \sqrt{1.0\times10^{6}}
     \approx 1.0\times10^{3}\ \text{V m}^{-1}.
\]

For a plane wave \(B_{0}=E_{0}/c\):

\[
B_{0}= \frac{1.0\times10^{3}}{3.0\times10^{8}}
      \approx 3.3\times10^{-6}\ \text{T}
      = 3.3\times10^{-6}\ \text{Wb m}^{-2}.
\]

Thus the amplitudes are \(E_{0}\approx 1.01\times10^{3}\ \text{V m}^{-1}\) and  
\(B_{0}\approx 3.37\times10^{-6}\ \text{Wb m}^{-2}\).

