The photoelectric equation is  

\[
K_{\max}= \frac{hc}{\lambda}-\phi .
\]

For the given blue light \(\lambda =4.1\times10^{-7}\,\text{m}\),

\[
E_{\text{photon}}=\frac{hc}{\lambda}
   =\frac{(6.626\times10^{-34}\,\text{J·s})(3.00\times10^{8}\,\text{m/s})}
          {4.1\times10^{-7}\,\text{m}}
   \approx 4.85\times10^{-19}\,\text{J}.
\]

The kinetic energy of the emitted electron is \(K_{\max}=3.2\times10^{-19}\,\text{J}\), therefore the work function is  

\[
\phi =E_{\text{photon}}-K_{\max}
      \approx 4.85\times10^{-19}-3.2\times10^{-19}
      =1.65\times10^{-19}\,\text{J}.
\]

The longest wavelength that can just liberate an electron (\(K_{\max}=0\)) satisfies  

\[
\phi =\frac{hc}{\lambda_{\max}}\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
\lambda_{\max}= \frac{hc}{\phi}
    =\frac{1.99\times10^{-25}\,\text{J·m}}{1.65\times10^{-19}\,\text{J}}
    \approx 1.2\times10^{-6}\,\text{m}.
\]

These values are closest to the pair \( \phi =1.7\times10^{-19}\,\text{J}\) and \(\lambda_{\max}=1.20\times10^{-6}\,\text{m}\).

