response:
The Clausius‑Clapeyron equation for a phase change can be written for a small interval around the normal boiling point as  

\[
\frac{dP}{dT}= \frac{\Delta H_{\mathrm{vap}}}{T\;\Delta V}
\]

For vaporization the liquid volume is negligible, so \(\Delta V\approx V_{\rm g}\). Assuming ideal‑gas behaviour,  

\[
V_{\rm g}= \frac{RT}{P}
\]

Thus  

\[
\frac{dP}{dT}= \frac{\Delta H_{\mathrm{vap}}\,P}{R\,T^{2}}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\Delta H_{\mathrm{vap}} = \frac{dP}{dT}\frac{R T^{2}}{P}
\]

At the normal boiling point \(T = 80^{\circ}\!C = 353\;{\rm K}\) and \(P=1\;{\rm atm}\).  
\(dP/dT = 0.030\;{\rm atm\,K^{-1}}\) and \(R = 0.082057\;{\rm L\,atm\, mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}\).

\[
\Delta H_{\mathrm{vap}} = 0.030\;\frac{{\rm atm}}{K}\;
      \frac{0.082057\;{\rm L\,atm\, mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}\;(353\;K)^{2}}{1\;{\rm atm}}
      \approx 3.07\times10^{2}\;{\rm L\,atm\, mol^{-1}}
\]

Since \(1\;{\rm L\,atm}=101.3\;{\rm J}\),

\[
\Delta H_{\mathrm{vap}}\approx 3.07\times10^{2}\times101.3\;{\rm J\,mol^{-1}}
                \approx 3.11\times10^{4}\;{\rm J\,mol^{-1}}
                \approx 7.4\;{\rm kcal\,mol^{-1}}
\]

The value closest to this is 7.4 kcal mol⁻¹.

