The Clausius–Clapeyron relation for a phase change is  

\[
\frac{d\ln P}{dT}= \frac{\Delta H_{\text{vap}}}{RT^{2}}
\]

At the normal boiling point the vapor pressure is 1 atm, so  

\[
\frac{d\ln P}{dT}= \frac{1}{P}\frac{dP}{dT}= \frac{0.030\; \text{atm K}^{-1}}{1\;\text{atm}}=0.030\;\text{K}^{-1}
\]

The boiling temperature is \(T = 80^{\circ}\text{C}=353\ \text{K}\). Using \(R = 1.987\ \text{cal mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}\):

\[
\Delta H_{\text{vap}} = R T^{2}\frac{d\ln P}{dT}
 = 1.987\; \text{cal mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}\times (353\ \text{K})^{2}\times 0.030\;\text{K}^{-1}
\]

\[
(353)^{2}=1.246\times10^{5}
\]
\[
1.987 \times 1.246\times10^{5}\times 0.030 \approx 7.4\times10^{3}\ \text{cal mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus \(\Delta H_{\text{vap}} \approx 7.4\ \text{kcal mol}^{-1}\).

