The power transmitted is  

\[
P = \frac{\Delta T \; v}{33\,000}\;\text{hp}
\]

where \(v\) is the belt speed.  
For a 1‑ft (12 in) pulley at 800 rpm  

\[
v = \pi D N = \pi(1\text{ ft})(800)=800\pi\; \text{ft/min}=2.513\times10^{3}\ \text{ft/min}
\]

Hence  

\[
\Delta T = \frac{P\;33\,000}{v}= \frac{25\,(33\,000)}{2.513\times10^{3}}
            \approx 3.28\times10^{2}\; \text{lb}
\]

The Euler belt formula gives the relation between tight‑side tension \(T_1\) and slack‑side tension \(T_2\):

\[
\frac{T_1}{T_2}=e^{\mu\theta},\qquad 
\mu =0.45,\; \theta =160^{\circ}=2.793\ \text{rad}
\]

\[
e^{\mu\theta}=e^{0.45(2.793)}\approx 3.514
\]

\[
\Delta T =T_1-T_2=T_2(e^{\mu\theta}-1)\;\Longrightarrow\;
T_2=\frac{\Delta T}{e^{\mu\theta}-1}
        =\frac{328}{2.514}\approx 131\ \text{lb}
\]

\[
T_1 = T_2 e^{\mu\theta}\approx 131(3.514)=459\ \text{lb}
\]

The belt must sustain this maximum tension.  With belt thickness \(t =0.25\) in and allowable stress \(\sigma_{allow}=550\) psi,

\[
\sigma = \frac{T_1}{b\,t}\le \sigma_{allow}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
b = \frac{T_1}{\sigma_{allow}\,t}
    =\frac{459}{550(0.25)}\approx 3.34\ \text{in}
\]

Choosing the nearest standard width gives **3.5 in**.

**ANSWER: H**

