\[
\text{mass per nucleus}= \frac{39\ \text{g}}{6.02\times10^{23}}
                     \approx 6.5\times10^{-23}\ \text{g}
\]

The radius of a nucleus is \(r = 4\times10^{-13}\,\text{cm}\).
The volume of a sphere is  

\[
V = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^{3}
   = \frac{4}{3}\pi (4\times10^{-13}\,\text{cm})^{3}
   = \frac{4}{3}\pi \,64\times10^{-39}\,\text{cm}^{3}
   \approx 2.7\times10^{-37}\ \text{cm}^{3}.
\]

Hence the density is  

\[
\rho = \frac{m}{V}
     = \frac{6.5\times10^{-23}\,\text{g}}{2.7\times10^{-37}\,\text{cm}^{3}}
     \approx 2.4\times10^{14}\,\text{g cm}^{-3}.
\]

One metric ton is \(10^{6}\) g, so  

\[
\rho = \frac{2.4\times10^{14}\ \text{g}}{10^{6}\ \text{g/ton}}
     \approx 2.4\times10^{8}\ \text{tons cm}^{-3}.
\]

The answer closest to this value is \(2.7 \times 10^{8}\) tons cm\(^{-3}\).

